# Application-specific configuration.
#
# This file is the Kconfig root for the application, so it must pull in
# Zephyr's own tree first; otherwise every Zephyr symbol is undefined.
#
# These are the values that a reviewer or a test build is expected to change
# without touching source. The 750 ms window, the 10 s deployment indication,
# the 5 s alarm and the 500 ms blink period are *not* configurable: they are
# the behaviour the assignment specifies.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

menu "Rocket demo"

config APP_APOGEE_MIN_ALT_CM
	int "Minimum altitude above the baseline before deploying (cm)"
	range 10 3000
	default 150
	help
	  Threshold relative to the baseline P0 captured at start-up, not to sea
	  level. 150 cm is about 1.7x the altitude noise measured on the bench
	  (0.89 m peak-to-peak). A bench value, not a flight value: a real vehicle
	  would set this well above the ground handling envelope.

config APP_APOGEE_MIN_DESCENT_MM_S
	int "Minimum average descent over 750 ms (mm/s)"
	range 0 20000
	default 300
	help
	  How far below zero the 750 ms average velocity must be. 0 is the literal
	  "becomes negative" of the assignment; 300 mm/s is a noise guard above it,
	  not a performance threshold.

	  The same value is applied to the least-squares slope over the same window.
	  Both estimators must be at least this far below zero, so a single bad edge
	  sample cannot pass the trigger on its own. On a ballistic fall the two are
	  mathematically identical, so the extra condition costs nothing there.

	  Timing: falling from rest at apogee, a 0.3 m/s trailing average is
	  reached about 0.41 s later, roughly 0.81 m below the peak. Raising this
	  value delays detection; it does not improve it in flight.

	  Note that a moving vehicle is not a bench: in the lab, a hand settle after
	  a fast lift reaches about 0.36 m/s, which is why bench.conf needs a
	  *larger* margin than this default.

config APP_APOGEE_MIN_DROP_CM
	int "Descent below the peak before deploying (cm)"
	range 0 500
	default 25
	help
	  How far the altitude must fall below the estimated peak, which is the
	  maximum of a three-sample median rather than the raw maximum, so an
	  isolated spike cannot become the peak.

	  25 cm is a confirmation guard, not a requirement. On a ballistic fall it
	  is free: 0.81 m has already been fallen when the 0.30 m/s average is
	  reached. On a bench it is the weakest guard, because a hand settle after
	  a fast lift measures 35-44 cm; bench.conf therefore uses a larger margin
	  rather than relying on this filter.

config APP_REARM_BUTTON
	bool "Re-arm a simulated flight from the user button"
	default y
	help
	  Pressing the button clears the detector history and latch, ends the
	  deployment indication and restarts the baseline calibration, so a new
	  simulated flight can begin without a reboot. Heat is deliberately left
	  alone: a hot sensor is a condition, not a flight artefact.

	  The pin is declared in app.overlay as rearm_button (PA0, active low,
	  pull-up). Disable this if the pin is needed for something else; a reset
	  is then a power cycle or a reflash.

config APP_REARM_DEBOUNCE_MS
	int "Re-arm button debounce (ms)"
	depends on APP_REARM_BUTTON
	default 50
	range 5 500
	help
	  How long the pressed level must be stable before a press is accepted.
	  A mechanical button bounces for a few milliseconds; the apogee thread
	  polls every 50 ms, so 50 ms costs at most one extra poll.

config APP_HEAT_THRESHOLD_MC
	int "Overheat threshold (milli-degrees Celsius)"
	range -40000 85000
	default 30000
	help
	  The temperature at or above which the overheat alarm starts. 30000 is
	  30.0 C: above a still bench, which reads about 23 C here, and reachable
	  by pinching the sensor.

config APP_HEAT_HYSTERESIS_MC
	int "Overheat release hysteresis (milli-degrees Celsius)"
	range 0 5000
	default 200
	help
	  The alarm clears only once the temperature falls below the threshold
	  minus this band. 200 = 0.2 C, which is above the sensor's noise so the
	  alarm does not chatter at the boundary.

config APP_REPLAY
	bool "Replay a recorded trace instead of the live sensor"
	default n
	help
	  Publishes a trace from traces/ through the normal measurement store, so
	  altitude, apogee, heat and the LED run unmodified. Exactly one publisher
	  exists per boot: the sensor thread is not started in this mode.

choice APP_REPLAY_TRACE
	prompt "Trace to replay"
	depends on APP_REPLAY
	default APP_TRACE_AT05
	help
	  One trace per build, so each acceptance scenario is reproducible and its
	  evidence is tied to a known binary. See traces/.

config APP_TRACE_AT04A
	bool "at04a - descending below the minimum"
config APP_TRACE_AT04B
	bool "at04b - above the minimum but ascending"
config APP_TRACE_AT05
	bool "at05 - ascent, peak, sustained descent"
config APP_TRACE_AT08
	bool "at08 - deployment then heat, cooling before the deadline"
config APP_TRACE_AT09
	bool "at09 - heat starts after the deployment deadline"
config APP_TRACE_AT07A
	bool "at07a - heat only, one 5 s window"
config APP_TRACE_AT07B
	bool "at07b - heat across renewals, then cooling"
endchoice

config APP_READY_INDICATION
	bool "Flash the LED twice when the system is ready"
	default y
	help
	  Once the sensor is answering, the baseline is frozen and the 750 ms
	  history is full, flash the LED twice (100 ms on, 120 ms off, 100 ms on).
	  This exists for untethered runs where no console is available.
	  It is not part of the assignment's LED behaviour; disable it to keep the
	  LED strictly for deployment and overheating.

endmenu
